### What is the problem?

Given a source string `s` and a target string `t`, find the shortest contiguous substring of `s` that contains all characters of `t`, including duplicates. If `t` has two A's, the window must contain at least two A's. If no such window exists, return an empty string.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MinimumWindowSubstring {
    public static String minWindow(String s, String t) {
        if (s == null || t == null || s.length() < t.length()) return "";
        Map<Character, Integer> need = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) need.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);

        Map<Character, Integer> window = new HashMap<>();
        int required = need.size();
        int formed = 0;
        int left = 0, bestStart = 0, bestLen = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char rc = s.charAt(right);
            window.merge(rc, 1, Integer::sum);
            if (need.containsKey(rc) && window.get(rc).intValue() == need.get(rc).intValue()) {
                formed++;
            }
            while (formed == required) {
                if (right - left + 1 < bestLen) {
                    bestLen = right - left + 1;
                    bestStart = left;
                }
                char lc = s.charAt(left);
                window.merge(lc, -1, Integer::sum);
                if (need.containsKey(lc) && window.get(lc) < need.get(lc)) {
                    formed--;
                }
                left++;
            }
        }
        return bestLen == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? "" : s.substring(bestStart, bestStart + bestLen);
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

The National Bank of India's incident response team has a long event-code log and a set of required event codes that together signal a specific failure pattern. The team wants the shortest contiguous log slice containing all required codes so they can zoom straight to the root cause without reading the entire log.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.MinimumWindowSubstring;

public class IncidentService {
    public void findNarrowestIncident(String log, String requiredCodes) {
        String window = MinimumWindowSubstring.minWindow(log, requiredCodes);
        System.out.println("Log: " + log);
        System.out.println("Required: " + requiredCodes);
        System.out.println("Narrowest slice: '" + window + "' (length " + window.length() + ")");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new IncidentService().findNarrowestIncident("ADOBECODEBANC", "ABC");
    }
}
```

```text
Log: ADOBECODEBANC
Required: ABC
Narrowest slice: 'BANC' (length 4)
```

### How does the expand-then-shrink pattern work?

First build the `need` map from the target. Then scan the source from left to right. For each character added at the right edge, update the window map and check if a required character just reached its needed count. When all required characters are satisfied (formed equals required), start shrinking from the left: record the window if it is the smallest so far, remove the leftmost character, and if that drops a required character below its needed count, decrement formed.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class ExpandShrinkDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "XAYBZCA";
        String t = "ABC";
        Map<Character, Integer> need = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) need.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        Map<Character, Integer> win = new HashMap<>();
        int required = need.size(), formed = 0, left = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char rc = s.charAt(right);
            win.merge(rc, 1, Integer::sum);
            if (need.containsKey(rc) && win.get(rc).intValue() == need.get(rc).intValue()) formed++;
            if (formed == required) {
                System.out.println("Valid window at [" + left + "," + right + "]: '" + s.substring(left, right + 1) + "'");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Valid window at [0,6]: 'XAYBZCA'
```

### How does the formed counter keep it linear?

The formed counter eliminates the need to rescan the window on every step. It only changes when a character crosses its required threshold on the way in or out. Characters not in the need map are added to the window map but never affect formed. This gives O(1) validity checks per step.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class FormedCounterNote {
    // Without formed counter: O(n * distinct_chars) to check validity each step
    // With formed counter: O(1) per step, only updates on threshold crossings
    //
    // formed++ when: need.containsKey(c) && window.get(c) == need.get(c)
    // formed-- when: need.containsKey(c) && window.get(c) < need.get(c)
    //
    // The equality check is what handles duplicate requirements correctly.
    // If target needs 2 A's, formed increments only when window reaches exactly 2.

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("formed counter makes each step O(1)");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

If the target is longer than the source, no valid window exists. If the target is empty, the result is an empty string. If the target has duplicate characters, the need map holds the required count for each, and formed only increments when that exact count is reached.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MinimumWindowSubstring.minWindow("A", "AA"));    // "" (not enough A's)
        System.out.println(MinimumWindowSubstring.minWindow("AA", "AA"));   // "AA"
        System.out.println(MinimumWindowSubstring.minWindow("ABC", ""));    // ""
        System.out.println(MinimumWindowSubstring.minWindow("A", "A"));     // "A"
    }
}
```

```text

AA

A
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using a set instead of a count map, which silently fails when the target has duplicate characters. The second mistake is incrementing formed on every occurrence rather than only when the exact threshold is crossed. The third mistake is comparing Integer objects with `==` instead of `.intValue()` or `.equals()`.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: uses == on Integer objects (fails for values > 127 due to caching)
    public static boolean buggyCheck(Map<Character, Integer> win, Map<Character, Integer> need, char c) {
        return win.get(c) == need.get(c); // reference comparison, not value!
    }

    // CORRECT: uses .intValue() or .equals()
    public static boolean correctCheck(Map<Character, Integer> win, Map<Character, Integer> need, char c) {
        return win.get(c).intValue() == need.get(c).intValue();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Character, Integer> win = new HashMap<>();
        Map<Character, Integer> need = new HashMap<>();
        win.put('A', 200); need.put('A', 200);
        System.out.println("Buggy:   " + buggyCheck(win, need, 'A'));   // may be false!
        System.out.println("Correct: " + correctCheck(win, need, 'A')); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy:   false
Correct: true
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(|s| + |t|) where |s| is the source length and |t| is the target length. Building the need map takes O(|t|). The sliding window traverses the source once with each pointer, and each character enters and leaves the window at most once. Space is O(|s| + |t|) for the two frequency maps.

### How is this different from the anagram sliding window?

The minimum window substring allows extra characters and variable window size. The anagram problem uses a fixed window size equal to the pattern length and requires an exact match. Both use the formed counter trick, but the minimum window expands and contracts freely while the anagram window slides by exactly one position.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you return all minimum windows, not just one?
- How would you handle the case where the target has characters not in the source?
- Can you solve this with an array instead of a HashMap for ASCII characters?
- How does this relate to the substring with concatenation of all words problem?
